Description: Store the state of a session's toplevel whenever its window changes.
 The protocol says the compositor updates the state of a toplevel added to a session whenever it changes, but
 mutter stores it only when the window is unmanaged, and with save-toplevels-on-session-destroy.patch when the
 session is destroyed. When the compositor ends without unmanaging its windows, as when GNOME Shell crashes or the
 machine resets or loses power, every window comes back at the next start where it was the last time it was
 stored, possibly days earlier, or opens unplaced if it never was.
 .
 The state of a toplevel is now stored whenever its window moves, changes its size, its maximized, fullscreen or
 minimized state, or its workspace, which covers everything meta_session_state_save_window stores, the tiled
 state and the monitor included. The session manager already writes the session file 3 seconds after the first
 change it is given, so a burst of changes, such as an interactive move, costs one write. Nothing is stored for a
 window without a workspace, which is not placed yet or shown on all workspaces, nor for a toplevel removed from
 its session with remove_toplevel. A restored toplevel is connected only once its stored state has been applied,
 which changes the window step by step and would otherwise replace that state while it is read.
 .
 The test restore-changed has a second client restore the session while the first one still has its window
 mapped, after the window was moved, which fails without the change.
Author: Mitar
Bug: https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/mutter/-/work_items/5181
Forwarded: no
Last-Update: 2026-11-04
